    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Pool Deck Sealer", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-pool-deck-sealer/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best pool deck sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a concrete or paver deck, a water-based penetrating silane-siloxane such as Foundation Armor SX5000 WB is the best choice: it protects without forming a film, so traction underfoot is unchanged. For travertine or natural stone surrounds, a stone impregnator like Miracle 511 is the right chemistry.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does sealing a pool deck make it slippery?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A penetrating sealer does not — it leaves nothing on the surface, so grip is unchanged. A film-forming or wet-look sealer absolutely can, and on a barefoot surface that is a safety issue rather than an inconvenience. If you use a topical sealer around a pool, have a slip-resistant additive mixed in.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How often should a pool deck be sealed?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Premium penetrating sealers typically last three to five years around a pool, less than they would elsewhere because of constant wetting and chlorine exposure. Budget products want reapplying every year or two. Test with water: if it soaks in and darkens the deck rather than beading, it is time.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does chlorine damage a pool deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Chlorinated water splashing onto unsealed concrete accelerates surface deterioration and leaves mineral staining as it evaporates. Salt water systems are harder still on masonry, because salt crystallising inside the pore structure causes spalling in the same way freeze-thaw does. Sealing slows both by keeping the water out of the pores.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you seal a pool deck yourself?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes — penetrating sealers go on with a pump sprayer and a roller, and the work itself is straightforward. The demanding parts are cleaning thoroughly first, letting the deck dry completely, and choosing a settled dry window so nothing gets rained on or splashed while curing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What about a travertine pool deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Travertine is porous and calcium-based, so it stains readily from sunscreen and body oils and etches from anything acidic. Use a penetrating stone impregnator rather than a concrete sealer, and never a topical gloss — a wet travertine deck with a film on it is exactly the wrong surface to walk on barefoot.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will sealing stop the deck getting hot?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Surface temperature is driven by colour and material, not by sealer. A dark deck in full sun will be hot whether sealed or not, and a glossy film can make it marginally worse. If heat is the problem, the fix is shade, a lighter surface, or a cool-deck coating designed for the purpose.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long before you can swim after sealing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Follow the product's cure time, which is typically 24 to 72 hours for foot traffic and longer before the deck should get wet. Keep splashing off a freshly sealed deck for the full window — water landing on a curing sealer is the most common cause of patchy or hazy results around pools.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
