          Everywhere else on a property, choosing between a penetrating sealer and a wet-look one is a matter of taste. Around a pool it is a matter of what happens when someone runs across it wet, and that changes the whole shape of the decision.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Why Pool Decks Are Different
        </h2>

        <p>
          A pool deck takes a combination of abuse that no other surface on a property sees at once.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Constant wetting and drying.</strong> Water is splashed onto the deck and evaporates repeatedly through the season, and every one of those cycles carries dissolved minerals into the surface and leaves them there.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Pool chemistry.</strong> Chlorinated water is harsher on masonry than rain, and salt water systems are harsher still. Salt crystallising inside the pore structure exerts pressure in the same way ice does, causing spalling over years.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Oils and sunscreen.</strong> Every product people put on their skin ends up on the deck. On porous surfaces — travertine especially — those stains soak in and are difficult to remove afterwards.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Bare feet.</strong> The one requirement that overrides everything: the surface has to stay grippy when wet and reasonably comfortable underfoot in the sun. That single constraint rules out most film-forming, glossy products regardless of how good they look dry.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What a Pool Deck Sealer Has to Do</p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Change to wet traction</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-5 yr</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Realistic life around a pool</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24-72 h</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cure before it gets wet</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">2</p>
              <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thin coats, not one heavy</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="slip" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. The Slip Problem
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the section that should decide your purchase, and it is the one most sealer marketing avoids.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>penetrating sealer</strong> lines the pores inside the concrete or stone and leaves nothing on the surface. The deck's texture — the broom finish, the exposed aggregate, the natural roughness of travertine — is completely unchanged, and so is how much grip a wet foot gets. This is why every recommendation on this page defaults to penetrating products around water.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>film-forming sealer</strong>, including every wet-look and gloss product, puts a smooth layer over that texture. Dry, it looks superb and the colour depth is genuinely striking. Wet, it is a smooth film with water on it, and the difference in traction is not subtle. Around a pool, where people are wet, barefoot and frequently in a hurry, that is a real hazard rather than a theoretical one.
        </p>

        <p>
          If a wet look is non-negotiable, the mitigation is a <strong>slip-resistant additive</strong> — a fine polymer or aggregate grit stirred into the sealer before application, which restores much of the lost traction with only a slight change in appearance. Add it to the whole batch, keep it suspended by stirring regularly during application, and treat it as mandatory rather than optional.
        </p>

        <p>
          Textured surfaces help but do not solve it. A sealed stamped deck with a deep pattern still has smooth, glossy high points that a foot lands on. The pattern buys you a little; the additive buys you considerably more.
        </p>

        <!-- Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Pool Deck Sealers, Reviewed
          </h2>

