                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-3">Cons</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-red-800">
                            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>
                            {con}
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-gray-100">
                    <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-1">The Bottom Line</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2 id="by-material" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. By Deck Material
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Broom-Finished Concrete</h3>
        <p>
          The most common pool deck and the most straightforward. A water-based silane-siloxane penetrating sealer protects against chlorine, salt and staining while leaving the broom texture doing its job. Nothing else is needed, and nothing glossy should go anywhere near it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Travertine and Natural Stone</h3>
        <p>
          Popular around pools because pale stone stays cooler underfoot, and porous enough that sunscreen and body oil staining is a genuine issue. Use a stone impregnator rather than a concrete sealer — the chemistry is matched to calcium-based stone. Our <a href="/best-natural-stone-sealer/">natural stone sealer guide</a> covers the wider category, including the etching that no sealer prevents.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pavers</h3>
        <p>
          A paver pool surround has the added complication of joints. Penetrating sealers leave joint sand alone; film-forming sealers can lock it, which sounds appealing and creates its own problems with trapped moisture and clouding. Our guide to <a href="/why-paver-sealer-turns-white/">why paver sealer turns white</a> covers that failure, which is more common around pools than anywhere else because the joints are so often damp.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stamped Concrete</h3>
        <p>
          The one material where the wet-look question genuinely bites, because stamped concrete is designed to be seen and the colour is usually protected by a film-forming sealer. Around a pool, the compromise is a matte or satin sealer with a slip-resistant additive rather than a high-gloss one. Our <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/">stamped concrete sealer guide</a> covers the range in more depth.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Textured Cool-Deck Coatings</h3>
        <p>
          Sprayed acrylic-cement textured coatings are a system rather than a bare surface, and they generally want the manufacturer's own maintenance product rather than a general sealer. Applying a penetrating concrete sealer over one usually achieves nothing, because there is no open concrete to penetrate.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the Deck Even Need Sealing?</h3>
        <p>
          Worth asking before spending anything, because some pool decks genuinely do not. The test is the same one used on any masonry: spray a little water on several areas and watch. If it soaks in and darkens the surface within about thirty seconds, the deck is absorbing and will take up chlorinated or salt water along with everything else. If it beads, either an existing sealer is still working or the surface is dense enough not to need one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Test in more than one place. The area nearest the water takes the most splash and wears first, while the far corner under a lounger may still be well protected. It is entirely normal for a deck to need resealing in a band around the pool long before the outer edges do, and treating only the worn zone is a legitimate approach as long as you use the same product.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other honest answer is that a sealer cannot fix a deck that has already deteriorated. Surface flaking, exposed aggregate where the paste has worn away, or a network of fine cracks all point to resurfacing rather than sealing. Sealing a failing surface preserves the failure, and around a pool the resulting rough patches are unpleasant underfoot as well as unattractive.
        </p>

        <h2 id="salt-vs-chlorine" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Salt Water vs Chlorine
        </h2>

        <p>
          Salt water pools are gentler on skin and considerably harder on masonry. Salt carried onto the deck in splashes and on wet feet crystallises inside the pore structure as the water evaporates, and those crystals exert pressure on the surrounding material exactly as ice does. Over years, that shows up as surface flaking and dusting on unsealed concrete — the same spalling pattern seen in freeze-thaw climates, arriving through a different route.
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical consequences are two. First, sealing matters more around a salt pool than a chlorinated one, because keeping water out of the pores also keeps the salt out. Second, expect a shorter interval between coats: salt exposure wears a sealer faster, and a deck that would go five years in a chlorinated setting may want attention at three.
        </p>

        <p>
          Rinsing the deck with fresh water occasionally through the season is a genuinely useful habit around a salt pool, particularly on the sides that take the most splash. It costs nothing and removes salt before it dries and crystallises.
        </p>

        <p>
          One further point specific to salt systems: the chlorinator cell and the associated plumbing occasionally leak a concentrated brine, and where that happens repeatedly on one spot of decking the damage is far worse than general splash. If a single area of the deck is deteriorating much faster than the rest, look at what is above or beside it before assuming the sealer failed — a slow drip of concentrated salt solution will defeat any coating on the market, and no amount of resealing fixes a plumbing fault.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Applying It Around a Pool
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean thoroughly and treat stains first.</strong> Sunscreen and oil marks need degreasing individually before the whole deck is washed, or you spread them. Organic staining and algae in shaded corners want a dedicated cleaner rather than a pressure washer alone.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Let it dry completely.</strong> A minimum of 24 hours after washing, and longer if the weather is cool or humid. A pool deck holds moisture at the edges and in shaded areas long after the middle looks dry.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Protect the water.</strong> Mask the coping edge and keep product out of the pool. Penetrating sealers are applied by sprayer, and overspray drifts more than people expect on a breezy day. Cover nearby planting and any painted metalwork as well, and work on a still morning rather than a windy afternoon.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Two thin coats, not one heavy one.</strong> Excess penetrating sealer that cannot absorb dries as a white haze on the surface — the single most common cosmetic failure, and one that takes real work to remove.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Respect the cure before anyone swims.</strong> Typically 24 to 72 hours before foot traffic and longer before the deck should get wet. Water landing on a curing sealer is the usual cause of patchy results, and around a pool it is very easy to arrange.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Timing the Job in the Season</h3>
        <p>
          The best window is at either end of the swimming season rather than the middle of it. Spring, before the pool is in heavy use, gives a settled dry spell in many climates and means the deck goes into summer protected. Autumn, once swimming has stopped, is equally good and has the advantage that nobody is waiting to get back in the water while the sealer cures.
        </p>
        <p>
          Whichever end you choose, the constraint is the same: two or three consecutive dry days, mild temperatures, and no swimming during the cure. Mid-season is the hardest time to arrange all of that, which is why so many pool decks get sealed hastily between two hot weekends and end up patchy. Plan it as a distinct job with the pool closed rather than something squeezed around normal use, and the result will look considerably better for the four or five years it lasts.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Gloss Sealer Without an Additive</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A glossy film on a wet deck walked on by bare feet is a genuine hazard. If the wet look matters, mix a slip-resistant additive into the whole batch and keep stirring during application.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Sunscreen Stains</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oils soak into porous decking and a sealer locks them in permanently. Degrease individual marks first, particularly on travertine, and accept that some may need a poultice and a second attempt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flooding It On for Better Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                More product does not mean more penetration. Anything the deck cannot absorb dries as a white haze on the surface, which is far harder to remove than it was to avoid.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Deck Get Wet Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Splashes on a curing sealer leave patches and haze. Keep everyone out of the pool for the full cure window, and pick a settled dry spell — this is not a job for a showery week.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a Salt Pool Deck Like a Chlorinated One</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Salt is harder on masonry and wears sealers faster. Around a salt pool, plan on a shorter reseal interval and rinse the splash zones with fresh water occasionally through the season.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best pool deck sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">For concrete or paver decks, a water-based penetrating silane-siloxane such as Foundation Armor SX5000 WB — it protects without forming a film, so traction is unchanged. For travertine or natural stone surrounds, a stone impregnator like Miracle 511 is the correct chemistry.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing make it slippery?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A penetrating sealer does not — nothing sits on the surface, so grip is unchanged. A film-forming or wet-look sealer can, and on a barefoot surface that is a safety issue. If you use a topical sealer around a pool, have a slip-resistant additive mixed in.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should it be resealed?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Premium penetrating sealers typically give three to five years around a pool — less than elsewhere, because of constant wetting and pool chemistry. Budget products want doing every year or two. Test with water: if it soaks in and darkens the deck rather than beading, it is due.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does chlorine damage the deck?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Chlorinated splash accelerates surface deterioration and leaves mineral staining as it evaporates. Salt systems are harder still, because salt crystallising in the pores causes spalling much as freeze-thaw does. Sealing slows both by keeping the water out of the pore structure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you do it yourself?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes — penetrating sealers go on with a pump sprayer and roller. The demanding parts are cleaning thoroughly, letting the deck dry completely, and choosing a settled dry window so nothing gets splashed or rained on while it cures.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What about travertine?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Use a penetrating stone impregnator, not a concrete sealer. Travertine is porous and calcium-based, so it stains from sunscreen and oils and etches from acids. Never use a topical gloss on it — a wet, filmed travertine deck is exactly the wrong barefoot surface.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will sealing stop it getting hot?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">No. Surface temperature comes from colour and material, not sealer, and a glossy film can make it marginally worse. If heat is the issue, the answers are shade, a lighter surface, or a cool-deck coating designed for the job.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before swimming?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Follow the cure time on the product — typically 24 to 72 hours before foot traffic and longer before the deck should get wet. Splashing on a curing sealer is the most common cause of patchy or hazy results around pools.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

