        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Around a pool, choose a <strong class="text-white">penetrating sealer</strong> — Foundation Armor SX5000 WB on concrete and pavers, Miracle 511 on travertine and stone. Both protect against chlorine, salt and staining while leaving the surface exactly as grippy as it was, which is the only specification that really matters on a deck people walk on wet and barefoot.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            If the wet look is worth having, buy it deliberately: a matte or satin film with a slip-resistant additive mixed through the whole batch, applied in two thin coats with a full cure before anyone swims. And around a salt pool, plan on resealing sooner and rinsing the splash zones through the season — salt does slowly what frost does quickly.
          </p>
